  "explains": "Four changed files have names that Next.js gives a meaning only in some places. src/components/layout.tsx (Frame) is a plain component, not a layout: layouts are layout files inside the app folder. It is imported by src/app/(shop)/cart/page.tsx, so /cart is reached by that import, and by src/app/_private/page.tsx, which is in a private folder (a folder that starts with an underscore is not routed), so no screen comes of it. src/lib/route.ts (buildRoute) and src/utils/middleware.ts (withLogging) are helpers, not a route handler and not middleware (middleware is the file beside the app folder, src/middleware.ts); both are imported by src/app/api/health/route.ts and used in GET, so GET /api/health is reached, with one chain for each import. The mobile client calls the health URL in ping, so the http edge goes to the route and StatusScreen (mounted at /status) is reached through the first chain. The change to the page in the private folder reaches nothing. Nothing is matched by file name alone: no other screen or route is reached, and in particular not every page (as a changed layout would) or every route (as changed middleware would).",
